dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: replacing a socket for an IPv6 proxy must not fail
on the source port

Applies on top of dante-ipv6-gateway.patch (and the other dante-*.patch
files).

socks_resocket() keeps the port of a socket the program bound to the IPv4
wildcard address with a fixed port, by binding the replacement IPv6 socket
to [::]:port. That port is free in IPv4, not necessarily in IPv6: e.g., a
program that binds every outgoing socket to "0.0.0.0" gets kernel-picked
ephemeral ports, and under load some of them are in use by IPv6 sockets.
The bind then failed, the connect to the proxy failed with EADDRINUSE, and
Rconnect() went into its "server socksified?" workaround. The replacement
socket only connects to the proxy server, where the source port does not
matter, so it is now left unbound when that bind fails. (The UDP data
socket keeps its own fallback to any port.)

Test: test/bindcheck.c (via test/run.sh).

--- a/lib/sockopt.c
+++ b/lib/sockopt.c
@@ -262,11 +262,17 @@
 
       SET_SOCKADDRPORT(&newlocal, GET_SOCKADDRPORT(&local));
 
-      if (bind(new_s, TOSA(&newlocal), salen(newlocal.ss_family)) == -1) {
-         swarn("%s: could not bind %s for fd %d",
-               function, sockaddr2string(&newlocal, NULL, 0), s);
-         goto fail;
-      }
+      if (bind(new_s, TOSA(&newlocal), salen(newlocal.ss_family)) == -1)
+         /*
+          * The port is free in the old family, not necessarily in the new
+          * one (e.g., an ephemeral port the kernel picked for the program's
+          * IPv4 socket).  The new socket only connects to the proxy server,
+          * where the source port does not matter: leave it unbound.
+          */
+         slog(LOG_DEBUG, "%s: could not bind %s for fd %d (%s), leaving the "
+                         "new socket unbound",
+              function, sockaddr2string(&newlocal, NULL, 0), s,
+              strerror(errno));
    }
    else if (IPADDRISBOUND(&local))
       slog(LOG_DEBUG,
